I mean, most of the history of our species

00:32:53.078 --> 00:32:55.200
was pre-written.

00:32:55.200 --> 00:32:56.963
It was oral.

00:32:58.211 --> 00:33:03.090
You got your stories via
an oral story teller.

00:33:03.090 --> 00:33:06.130
Right, it's just a very
recent concept the idea

00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:09.240
that you had to justify
that you're actually

00:33:09.240 --> 00:33:11.010
getting information because
it's been translated

00:33:11.010 --> 00:33:13.095
into words, screw that.

00:33:13.095 --> 00:33:14.460
I mean written words.

00:33:14.460 --> 00:33:16.160
- You're currently justifying it.

00:33:16.160 --> 00:33:18.420
- I'm definitely justifying
it, but I'm saying

00:33:18.420 --> 00:33:19.400
I don't qualify it anymore.

00:33:19.400 --> 00:33:21.483
- And you're saying
that you don't need to.

00:33:21.483 --> 00:33:22.440
- Yeah, exactly.

00:33:22.440 --> 00:33:25.404
- So live in Jesse's power.

00:33:25.404 --> 00:33:27.340
- You think my wife is--

00:33:27.340 --> 00:33:29.620
- I'm just trying to make that a runner,

00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:32.010
for the episode, that she's powerful.

00:33:32.010 --> 00:33:33.400
- Oh she's very powerful.

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:36.500
- But in contrast to you.

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:38.500
- I have no problem with admitting that.

00:33:39.381 --> 00:33:41.743
- So just listen to the books.

00:33:42.710 --> 00:33:44.350
- But here's what I'll say.

00:33:44.350 --> 00:33:47.290
- You've empowered many, me, Mari.

00:33:47.290 --> 00:33:52.290
- I'm listening, this
morning, listening to a book

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:53.477
while I worked out.

00:33:53.477 --> 00:33:56.743
I only listen to books while driving.

00:33:58.110 --> 00:34:00.800
I kind of feel like I made a choice,

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:03.201
I really like music, but
I only listen to music

00:34:03.201 --> 00:34:06.423
while I'm writing, while I'm working.

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:09.930
I don't listen to music
at any other time now.

00:34:11.223 --> 00:34:12.690
- I have to split my time.

00:34:12.690 --> 00:34:14.380
That's the thing for me with audio books,

00:34:14.380 --> 00:34:17.560
is that I love listening to music so much

00:34:17.560 --> 00:34:21.150
that I kind of have to
make a disciplined decision

00:34:21.150 --> 00:34:25.510
to continue to get into my book,

00:34:25.510 --> 00:34:29.428
so that's why walking, my
walks has become my book zone

00:34:29.428 --> 00:34:32.860
and there's not a temptation
to listen to music.

00:34:32.860 --> 00:34:34.530
So now,

00:34:34.530 --> 00:34:37.050
when I'm mountain biking by myself,

00:34:37.050 --> 00:34:40.997
I also, I can listen to
an audio book sometimes.

00:34:42.540 --> 00:34:45.050
- Usually on the uphill and
then on the downhill part

00:34:45.050 --> 00:34:46.910
I might switch to music.

00:34:46.910 --> 00:34:48.040
- That's interesting.

00:34:48.040 --> 00:34:50.683
Because, speaking of my powerful wife,

00:34:53.010 --> 00:34:56.660
she comes down and I like
to work out by myself.

00:34:56.660 --> 00:34:58.690
Let's just, I'll be
honest and she knows this,

00:34:58.690 --> 00:35:00.250
but you know there's only
so much time in the day

00:35:00.250 --> 00:35:02.180
for the both of us so sometimes
we're both down there.

00:35:02.180 --> 00:35:04.240
Now what I do when I'm working out,

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:07.860
is I listen to an audio book and sometimes

00:35:07.860 --> 00:35:12.860
I listen to a very dry,
like academic audio book

00:35:13.710 --> 00:35:15.840
about some scientific concept
or something like that

00:35:15.840 --> 00:35:17.877
while I'm sitting there, going rargh!

00:35:18.850 --> 00:35:20.320
And she comes down there
and she laughs at me,

00:35:20.320 --> 00:35:22.490
and she's like, how do you do this?

00:35:22.490 --> 00:35:27.480
How do you work out hard
and listen to this professor

00:35:27.480 --> 00:35:28.780
talk about this thing?

00:35:28.780 --> 00:35:30.260
- Yeah, it's a juxtaposition.

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:31.730
- She's like, I got to have music.

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:33.870
And she comes down and of
course she's the powerful one,

00:35:33.870 --> 00:35:34.970
so we listen to music.

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:38.000
And when we're listening to music,

00:35:38.000 --> 00:35:40.520
I feel like I'm missing out on something.

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:42.410
I'm like, man, I had an hour here,

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:43.243
and I was--

00:35:43.243 --> 00:35:44.076
- Learning something.

00:35:44.076 --> 00:35:44.909
- And if she wasn't here,

00:35:44.909 --> 00:35:47.470
I would be making my body better and also

00:35:47.470 --> 00:35:49.460
making my mind better,
but now I'm just listening

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:52.879
to dribble, I'm listening
to Alicia Keys say,

00:35:52.879 --> 00:35:54.737
"This girl is on fire!"

00:35:56.327 --> 00:35:57.160
As I like--

00:35:57.160 --> 00:35:58.290
- Is Jessie doing that?

00:35:58.290 --> 00:36:01.660
- Yeah, which by the
way is an excellent song

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:04.160
to work out to, I understand.

00:36:04.160 --> 00:36:06.300
- Yeah, it's got a big beat.

00:36:06.300 --> 00:36:08.840
- And then there's that,
Jessie has put together

00:36:08.840 --> 00:36:10.900
this playlist and it's got something,

00:36:10.900 --> 00:36:11.733
it's not current.

00:36:11.733 --> 00:36:15.450
You know when Gwen Stefani hit that stride

00:36:15.450 --> 00:36:20.450
and was like, I don't know when it was,

00:36:20.730 --> 00:36:22.390
it was like 10 years ago.

00:36:22.390 --> 00:36:24.877
- On that song with Eve where it's like...

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:29.199
- Yeah, yeah, that's good to work out too.

00:36:29.199 --> 00:36:30.877
♪ Let me blow your mind ♪

00:36:30.877 --> 00:36:33.410
- Yeah, and so she's
got that kind of thing.

00:36:33.410 --> 00:36:38.360
- And I get this, I start moving,

00:36:38.360 --> 00:36:40.240
but then I'm just like, man
I can't want to get to my car

00:36:40.240 --> 00:36:41.360
to learn something.

00:36:41.360 --> 00:36:42.797
- You're addicted to ideas.

00:36:42.797 --> 00:36:44.594
- Yeah, definitely.

00:36:44.594 --> 00:36:48.780
- Think about it.

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:51.560
- Think about it, you're gonna
get yourself into trouble.

00:36:51.560 --> 00:36:53.740
- So what are we talking about?

00:36:53.740 --> 00:36:54.823
Audio books.

00:36:56.245 --> 00:36:57.833
- I can't live without 'em.

00:36:57.833 --> 00:37:00.510
Of course I could live
without 'em, but I see them

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:03.310
as a necessary part of
my life at this point.

00:37:03.310 --> 00:37:07.260
- Rachel said, popsocket.

00:37:07.260 --> 00:37:09.970
I thought they were so dumb till I got one

00:37:09.970 --> 00:37:12.090
and I can't hold my phone without it.

00:37:12.090 --> 00:37:15.360
Helps when I'm fidgety, but I
don't switch out the designs.

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:16.310
Which you could do.

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:19.042
- You popsocketed for a while.

00:37:19.042 --> 00:37:24.042
- I did and my phone case
broke and I haven't gotten

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:27.560
a new one because I thought
I was gonna get a new phone,

00:37:27.560 --> 00:37:30.890
so instead of getting a
new case, I just waited

00:37:30.890 --> 00:37:33.620
until I got a new phone,
but I never got a new phone,

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:37.920
so now I have a caseless
phone and it's slicker

00:37:37.920 --> 00:37:38.970
because there's no case.

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:40.080
- I don't know how you live like that.

00:37:40.080 --> 00:37:41.330
- And I was thinking about,

00:37:41.330 --> 00:37:42.740
instead of getting a case,

00:37:42.740 --> 00:37:44.290
just getting a popsocket.

00:37:44.290 --> 00:37:45.830
Hey, we sell those.

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:48.603
Mythical.com, get your mythical popsocket.

00:37:50.400 --> 00:37:52.950
You might think it's stupid
until you try it out.

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:53.797
- I tried it out for a while.

00:37:53.797 --> 00:37:55.900
- I've got to go back to it
because I've noticed that,

00:37:55.900 --> 00:37:58.470
here's the thing, I hold
the phone like this,

00:37:58.470 --> 00:38:01.997
and so my pinky is below the phone

00:38:01.997 --> 00:38:06.640
and it turns out there is,

00:38:06.640 --> 00:38:07.550
you know,

00:38:07.550 --> 00:38:09.470
you think you're holding
it in random places,

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:12.440
but it goes to the exact same place,

00:38:12.440 --> 00:38:14.910
I hold my phone in the exact same way.

00:38:14.910 --> 00:38:15.853
- Right on the vein?

00:38:15.853 --> 00:38:20.810
- Yeah, I pinch the vein, now
I'm getting a pain right here

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:22.360
on my pinky finger.

00:38:22.360 --> 00:38:23.260
This isn't funny

00:38:24.870 --> 00:38:26.068
and literally it hurts.

00:38:26.068 --> 00:38:28.500
When I got to my instinctive location

00:38:28.500 --> 00:38:29.600
to look at my phone,

00:38:29.600 --> 00:38:32.760
my pinky finger, there's a pain spot.

00:38:32.760 --> 00:38:34.010
And I need a popsocket.

00:38:34.010 --> 00:38:35.810
- No, look, rubber.

00:38:35.810 --> 00:38:37.900
I did the same thing,
but mine doesn't hurt

00:38:37.900 --> 00:38:39.710
'cause I've got a little
rubber protective little thing.

00:38:39.710 --> 00:38:42.090
- And you know what I
think that's what it is.

00:38:42.090 --> 00:38:44.240
It didn't hurt until my,
my case has been gone

00:38:44.240 --> 00:38:45.082
long enough that I've developed--

00:38:45.082 --> 00:38:46.210
- You don't need a
popsocket, you need a case-

00:38:46.210 --> 00:38:47.093
- Carpal tunnel.

00:38:49.005 --> 00:38:50.203
We don't sell cases.

00:38:51.400 --> 00:38:52.233
- Maybe we should.

00:38:52.233 --> 00:38:54.640
- We sell popsockets, but let me just say,

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:56.057
this is truth in advertising,

00:38:56.057 --> 00:38:58.976
I don't use them and I don't--

00:38:58.976 --> 00:39:02.200
- That's not what truth in advertising is.

00:39:02.200 --> 00:39:05.530
When you say you sell
something but you don't use it.

00:39:05.530 --> 00:39:06.880
- It is truth in advertising.

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:08.940
- Okay, you're being
truthful about something

00:39:08.940 --> 00:39:10.900
that we advertise, but you don't use.

00:39:10.900 --> 00:39:13.670
- I'm saying, I want you
to have the opportunity

00:39:13.670 --> 00:39:15.540
to buy a mythical popsocket.

00:39:15.540 --> 00:39:16.373
- If you're into that.

00:39:16.373 --> 00:39:21.120
- But I don't endorse them
personally or use them.

00:39:21.120 --> 00:39:23.870
I just think that what happens
to me with the popsocket

00:39:25.270 --> 00:39:28.310
is it makes it, it gets caught on.

00:39:28.310 --> 00:39:31.480
I just want to get this thing
in and out of my pocket,

00:39:31.480 --> 00:39:32.940
as quickly as possible,

00:39:32.940 --> 00:39:35.700
but I want it to have a little resistance.

00:39:35.700 --> 00:39:37.850
I can't imagine, there's
been a couple of days

00:39:37.850 --> 00:39:39.361
where I've gone without a case,

00:39:39.361 --> 00:39:43.050
I have a case and a screen
protector, a glass--

00:39:43.050 --> 00:39:45.560
- Why don't they just make phones

00:39:45.560 --> 00:39:49.363
with the case as part of it?

00:39:50.260 --> 00:39:52.600
- Well because then you wouldn't
have the power of choice

00:39:52.600 --> 00:39:54.210
and also they wouldn't
be able to brag about

00:39:54.210 --> 00:39:55.210
how thin it is.

00:39:55.210 --> 00:39:57.567
- Make a phone good enough
that you don't need a case.

00:39:57.567 --> 00:40:02.567
- I mean that's how Neil
deGrasse Tyson famously

00:40:02.600 --> 00:40:06.430
doesn't have a phone case
and when asked about it,

00:40:06.430 --> 00:40:11.430
he will take his phone
and play around with it,

00:40:11.860 --> 00:40:14.660
and show, that he's like,
I'm not gonna drop this,

00:40:14.660 --> 00:40:15.630
why would I need a case?

00:40:15.630 --> 00:40:18.740
The only reason I'd need a
case if I needed the phone

00:40:18.740 --> 00:40:21.063
to be protected from me

00:40:21.063 --> 00:40:25.090
and I don't need to
protect my phone from me.

00:40:25.090 --> 00:40:28.030
But I, I'm not Neil deGrasse Tyson,

00:40:28.030 --> 00:40:29.342
I may be addicted to ides,

00:40:29.342 --> 00:40:31.830
but I'm not Neil deGrasse Tyson

00:40:31.830 --> 00:40:34.470
and I need a case because I
do need to protect my phone

00:40:34.470 --> 00:40:36.390
from myself, but I don't need a popsocket,

00:40:36.390 --> 00:40:38.600
but if you need one, more power to you.

00:40:38.600 --> 00:40:42.330
- Jafar says a memory foam pillow.

00:40:42.330 --> 00:40:45.340
I'm a tall fellow who has
tremendous back and neck issues

00:40:45.340 --> 00:40:48.070
and switching to this pillow
brought the pain I deal with

00:40:48.070 --> 00:40:50.553
in the morning to an
almost tolerable level,

00:40:50.553 --> 00:40:53.143
I have so much trouble
sleeping without it.

00:40:56.120 --> 00:40:59.750
The way you sleep is key to
dealing with chronic pain,

00:40:59.750 --> 00:41:01.250
but that can't be the extent of it.

00:41:01.250 --> 00:41:04.230
Jafar, I'm gonna point
you to Rhett's video

00:41:04.230 --> 00:41:07.250
where he shows you his stretching routine,

00:41:07.250 --> 00:41:08.970
he hasn't made it yet,

00:41:08.970 --> 00:41:12.010
but we're really laying
on the pressure now.

00:41:12.010 --> 00:41:13.160
- Here's what I'll say,

00:41:14.213 --> 00:41:15.870
I just went on the
internet and put together

00:41:15.870 --> 00:41:17.330
a bunch of exercises,

00:41:17.330 --> 00:41:20.100
it's not like I'm some stretching guru.

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:21.870
I do plan on making a video about it,

00:41:21.870 --> 00:41:24.160
but you can also just put together

00:41:24.160 --> 00:41:25.310
what feels good to you.

00:41:25.310 --> 00:41:29.830
- Now, I had neck pain,
when I went to my physical

00:41:29.830 --> 00:41:32.810
therapist who told me I needed to invite

00:41:32.810 --> 00:41:35.140
my rib to the party.

00:41:35.140 --> 00:41:36.590
I believe I also told you at the time

00:41:36.590 --> 00:41:41.590
that she sold me a
flax-seed filled cylindrical

00:41:42.615 --> 00:41:46.870
pillow that, I put it up on Amazon,

00:41:46.870 --> 00:41:50.390
this thing is called
sachi organic buck weed,

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:52.860
so it's not flax seed.

00:41:52.860 --> 00:41:54.260
- Yeah, flax seed is what you eat.

00:41:54.260 --> 00:41:55.670
- Cylinder neck pillow,

00:41:55.670 --> 00:41:57.700
I think some of them do have flax seed

00:41:57.700 --> 00:42:01.979
and I'm still using that
thing almost a year later.

00:42:01.979 --> 00:42:03.672
- That's the only pillow that you use?

00:42:03.672 --> 00:42:04.505
- Yeah.

00:42:04.505 --> 00:42:05.338
- So there's nothing else?

00:42:05.338 --> 00:42:06.171
- Nothing else.

00:42:06.171 --> 00:42:07.790
But I kind of hate it.

00:42:07.790 --> 00:42:08.979
- Do you sleep on your back or your side?

00:42:08.979 --> 00:42:09.812
- But I do think it works,

00:42:09.812 --> 00:42:13.370
it forces me to sleep on my back

00:42:13.370 --> 00:42:15.990
and it arches my--

00:42:15.990 --> 00:42:17.020
- Could you sleep on your side?

00:42:17.020 --> 00:42:21.240
- It pushes my chin down into my neck

00:42:21.240 --> 00:42:23.922
and it stretches out the back of my neck

00:42:23.922 --> 00:42:25.317
in such a way--

00:42:25.317 --> 00:42:27.770
- And it doesn't hurt when you wake up?

00:42:27.770 --> 00:42:31.380
- It kind of puts it in
traction in a good way.

00:42:31.380 --> 00:42:35.710
Oh yeah, the reviews are
really positive on Amazon,

00:42:35.710 --> 00:42:38.920
670 reviews, four and a half stars.

00:42:38.920 --> 00:42:42.210
I mean it costs like 37 bucks.

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:43.043
But I'll tell you,

00:42:43.043 --> 00:42:47.730
it took weeks of just
saying, you know what,

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:50.830
I'm committed to this thing and even now

00:42:50.830 --> 00:42:52.100
almost a year later,

00:42:52.100 --> 00:42:53.590
whenever I roll on my side,

00:42:53.590 --> 00:42:55.300
it's really not that comfortable.

00:42:55.300 --> 00:42:56.425
- So you immediately wake up?

00:42:56.425 --> 00:43:00.866
- I wake up enough to go back to my back,

00:43:00.866 --> 00:43:02.423
but that's kind of part of it.

00:43:03.580 --> 00:43:05.020
I guess this memory foam pillow,

00:43:05.020 --> 00:43:07.650
the way this one is shaped
for Jafar kind of helps.

00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:10.330
I'm seeing new ones
being pushed on Instagram

00:43:10.330 --> 00:43:12.900
right now, it's a memory foam pillow

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.110
with a hole in the middle basically

00:43:15.110 --> 00:43:17.320
that simulates the same posture

00:43:17.320 --> 00:43:19.327
that my seat pillow.

00:43:19.327 --> 00:43:21.410
- There's also the square pillow.

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:23.110
- Well that one also has,
if you sleep on your side.

00:43:23.110 --> 00:43:24.470
- That's a side sleep--

00:43:24.470 --> 00:43:27.700
- It has a big hole in the middle

00:43:27.700 --> 00:43:29.210
and two smaller holes on the side,

00:43:29.210 --> 00:43:30.810
so if you roll on your side,

00:43:30.810 --> 00:43:35.363
your ear is like suspended in a crater.

00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:38.110
I'm like okay, this sounds like a gimmick.

00:43:38.110 --> 00:43:42.043
I'm gonna stick with my buckweed pillow.

00:43:43.768 --> 00:43:45.180
I do think it's helped.

00:43:45.180 --> 00:43:47.620
- I told you about the
contraption that I bought

00:43:47.620 --> 00:43:49.620
that was designed to keep
you sleeping on your back

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:50.600
that had like,

00:43:50.600 --> 00:43:52.240
it was a belt that you wore

00:43:52.240 --> 00:43:54.730
that had these big plastic balls

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:55.960
so if you slept on your side,

00:43:55.960 --> 00:43:57.640
you'd be sleeping on top of a ball

00:43:57.640 --> 00:43:58.473
and you'd wake up.

00:43:58.473 --> 00:43:59.440
I actually tried it for about a week,

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:04.440
and I just, I'm a side sleeper,

00:44:04.441 --> 00:44:06.230
I want to be a back sleeper,

00:44:06.230 --> 00:44:09.880
I think it's the best for a
number of different reasons,

00:44:09.880 --> 00:44:13.820
but I don't know, I'm not
having trouble with my back

00:44:13.820 --> 00:44:16.730
or my neck because of
sleeping, so I'm kind of just

00:44:16.730 --> 00:44:17.563
on my side,

00:44:17.563 --> 00:44:19.830
but I want to get your take on this,

00:44:19.830 --> 00:44:24.830
'cause my resistance, my
resistance to a particular pillow.

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:30.480
I have two pillows,
they're both down pillows,

00:44:30.480 --> 00:44:34.300
but one of them is too like,

00:44:34.300 --> 00:44:35.690
your head goes all the way to the ground

00:44:35.690 --> 00:44:36.580
and that doesn't work.

